Here's a simple function in Go that calculates the square root of a given number:

```go
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "math"
)

// calculateSquareRoot calculates the square root of a given number
func calculateSquareRoot(num float64) float64 {
    if num < 0 {
        panic("Cannot calculate square root of a negative number")
    }
    return math.Sqrt(num)
}

func main() {
    // read in a number from the user
    var num float64
    fmt.Print("Enter a number: ")
    fmt.Scanln(&num)

    // calculate and print the square root
    sqrt := calculateSquareRoot(num)
    fmt.Printf("The square root of %.2f is %.2f\n", num, sqrt)
}
```

This code defines a function `calculateSquareRoot` that takes a `float64` number as input and returns its square root. It also checks if the input number is negative, in which case it panics with an error message. The `main` function reads in a number from the user, calls `calculateSquareRoot` to calculate the square root, and prints the result. 

To compile and run this code, save it to a file (e.g. `square_root.go`), navigate to the directory containing the file, and run `go run square_root.go`. Then, enter a number when prompted to see its square root. 

Note: This code uses the `math.Sqrt` function from the Go standard library to calculate the square root.

In the game Bitburner, the return value of a script is not directly accessible in the same way that it would be in a standard programming environment. 

However, you can achieve similar functionality by using the `eval()` function in Bitburner's `console` object. 

Here is an example of how you can use it:

```javascript
console.log(eval("2 + 2"));
```

This will print the result of the expression `2 + 2` to the console, which is `4`.

Alternatively, you can use the `eval()` function to execute a function and return its result. Here is an example:

```javascript
function add(a, b) {
    return a + b;
}

console.log(eval("add(2, 2)"));
```

This will print the result of the function `add(2, 2)`, which is `4`.

Keep in mind that using `eval()` can be a security risk if you're executing code that comes from an untrusted source, because it can evaluate any JavaScript expression. However, in the context of Bitburner, where you're executing your own code, it's generally safe.

Also note that the `eval()` function will throw an exception if the code you're evaluating contains syntax errors. If you want to handle this situation, you can use a try-catch block:

```javascript
try {
    console.log(eval("2 + 2"));
} catch (error) {
    console.log("Error:", error);
}
```

To create a new line within a cell in LaTeX table, you can use the `\\` command. Here is an example:

```latex
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
\hline
Text line 1 \\
Text line 2 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
```

However, if you want to keep the `\\` command within a cell, without splitting it across multiple cells, you can use the `array` package and its `m{}` column type, which allows for line breaks within a cell. Here is an example:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{array}

\begin{document}

\begin{tabular}{|m{5cm}|}
\hline
Text line 1 \\
Text line 2 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

\end{document}
```

Alternatively, you can use the `p{}` column type from the `array` package, but it requires a fixed height for the cell, which might not be what you want.

Another option is to use the `longtable` package, which allows for line breaks within a cell and also provides a way to continue a table over multiple pages.

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{longtable}

\begin{document}

\begin{longtable}{|p{5cm}|}
\hline
Text line 1 \\
Text line 2 \\
\hline
\end{longtable}

\end{document}
```

Note that you can adjust the `5cm` value to suit your needs.

If you want to have more control over the table and its alignment, you can use the `booktabs` package, which provides a more professional-looking table layout. However, it does not provide a built-in way to break lines within a cell.

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{booktabs}

\begin{document}

\begin{tabular}{|p{5cm}|}
\toprule
Text line 1 \\
Text line 2 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\end{document}
```
